const TRIVIA=[{"n":1,"q":"Who first reported the spontaneous radiation emitted by uranium salts in 1896?","opts":["Wilhelm Röntgen","Henri Becquerel","Ernest Rutherford","Niels Bohr"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Henri Becquerel","why":"Becquerel found that uranium compounds could expose photographic plates without an external light source, establishing natural radioactivity."},{"n":2,"q":"Marie and Pierre Curie named the element polonium after which place?","opts":["A district of Paris","Pierre Curie’s birthplace","Marie Curie’s native Poland","A mountain in Bohemia"],"a":2,"answer_text":"Marie Curie’s native Poland","why":"Polonium, announced in 1898, was named for Poland, which did not then exist as an independent state."},{"n":3,"q":"Which ore did the Curies process while searching for radium and polonium?","opts":["Bauxite","Pitchblende","Galena","Magnetite"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Pitchblende","why":"Pitchblende was more radioactive than its uranium content alone could explain, pointing to additional radioactive elements."},{"n":4,"q":"Which scientist distinguished alpha and beta radiation near the end of the nineteenth century?","opts":["Ernest Rutherford","James Chadwick","Enrico Fermi","Harold Urey"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Ernest Rutherford","why":"Rutherford showed that radioactive emissions included at least two components with different penetrating and deflection behavior."},{"n":5,"q":"Who discovered the highly penetrating radiation later called gamma rays?","opts":["Paul Villard","Hans Geiger","Frederick Soddy","Otto Hahn"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Paul Villard","why":"Villard identified the penetrating radiation in 1900; the alpha, beta, and gamma naming scheme became standard soon afterward."},{"n":6,"q":"By the early twentieth century, alpha particles had been identified as what?","opts":["Fast electrons","Helium nuclei","High-energy photons","Hydrogen molecules"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Helium nuclei","why":"Rutherford and collaborators established that alpha particles are doubly charged helium nuclei."},{"n":7,"q":"What did the Geiger-Marsden gold-foil experiment show about the atom?","opts":["Its positive charge fills the entire atom evenly","Most of its mass and positive charge occupy a tiny nucleus","Electrons have no mass","Atoms cannot be divided"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Most of its mass and positive charge occupy a tiny nucleus","why":"The rare large-angle deflections of alpha particles required a compact, massive, positively charged center."},{"n":8,"q":"What was the central innovation of Niels Bohr’s 1913 atomic model?","opts":["The nucleus contains only neutrons","Electrons occupy allowed, quantized energy states","Atoms are solid spheres","All elements have the same spectrum"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Electrons occupy allowed, quantized energy states","why":"Bohr used discrete electron states to explain why atoms emit and absorb light at specific wavelengths."},{"n":9,"q":"Henry Moseley’s X-ray measurements showed that an element’s fundamental ordering number is its what?","opts":["Atomic mass","Atomic number","Density","Melting point"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Atomic number","why":"Moseley connected characteristic X-ray frequencies to nuclear charge, placing atomic number on a physical foundation."},{"n":10,"q":"What does the term isotope describe?","opts":["Atoms with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons","Atoms with different protons but identical mass","Molecules with different shapes","Electrons in different energy states"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Atoms with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons","why":"Frederick Soddy introduced the isotope concept to explain chemically identical atoms with different radioactive or mass properties."},{"n":11,"q":"Which equation connected mass and energy before nuclear fission was discovered?","opts":["F = ma","E = mc²","PV = nRT","V = IR"],"a":1,"answer_text":"E = mc²","why":"Einstein’s 1905 mass-energy relation made clear that a small mass difference could correspond to a very large energy difference."},{"n":12,"q":"Rutherford’s 1919 transmutation experiment changed nitrogen into which element?","opts":["Carbon","Oxygen","Helium","Uranium"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Oxygen","why":"Bombarding nitrogen with alpha particles produced oxygen and a hydrogen nucleus, demonstrating artificial nuclear transmutation."},{"n":13,"q":"The hydrogen nucleus became known as which particle?","opts":["Neutron","Proton","Positron","Photon"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Proton","why":"Rutherford recognized the hydrogen nucleus as a fundamental positively charged constituent of atomic nuclei."},{"n":14,"q":"Who demonstrated the existence of the neutron in 1932?","opts":["James Chadwick","Arthur Compton","Ernest Lawrence","George de Hevesy"],"a":0,"answer_text":"James Chadwick","why":"Chadwick showed that penetrating radiation from beryllium consisted of neutral particles with approximately the proton’s mass."},{"n":15,"q":"Who discovered the positron in cosmic-ray tracks in 1932?","opts":["Carl Anderson","Paul Dirac","Walter Bothe","John Cockcroft"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Carl Anderson","why":"Anderson observed a positively charged particle with the electron’s mass, confirming the existence of antimatter predicted by Dirac."},{"n":16,"q":"Harold Urey’s “heavy hydrogen” is now called what?","opts":["Tritium","Deuterium","Helium-3","Protium oxide"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Deuterium","why":"Urey isolated hydrogen’s mass-2 isotope, whose nucleus contains one proton and one neutron."},{"n":17,"q":"Irène and Frédéric Joliot-Curie discovered artificial radioactivity by bombarding aluminum with what?","opts":["Alpha particles","Sound waves","Visible light","Protons from the solar wind"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Alpha particles","why":"Their 1934 experiment created a radioactive phosphorus isotope that continued emitting after the alpha source was removed."},{"n":18,"q":"What surprising effect did Enrico Fermi’s group find when some neutron experiments were performed near hydrogen-rich material such as paraffin?","opts":["Neutrons disappeared permanently","Slower neutrons could produce nuclear reactions more effectively","All radioactivity stopped","The target became chemically inert"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Slower neutrons could produce nuclear reactions more effectively","why":"Collisions with light nuclei slowed neutrons, and slow neutrons often had a greater probability of being captured."},{"n":19,"q":"What machine did Ernest Lawrence invent to accelerate charged particles repeatedly through a magnetic field?","opts":["Cloud chamber","Cyclotron","Mass spectrograph","Geiger counter"],"a":1,"answer_text":"Cyclotron","why":"The cyclotron used a magnetic field and alternating electric potential to accelerate particles in a spiral path."},{"n":20,"q":"Cockcroft and Walton’s 1932 accelerator experiment split lithium nuclei using which projectile?","opts":["Protons","Neutrons","Gamma rays","Electrons"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Protons","why":"Their high-voltage apparatus accelerated protons into lithium, producing alpha particles and demonstrating accelerator-driven nuclear disintegration."},{"n":21,"q":"Which instrument makes the paths of charged particles visible as condensation trails?","opts":["Wilson cloud chamber","Calorimeter","Mass balance","Diffraction grating"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Wilson cloud chamber","why":"C. T. R. Wilson’s chamber allowed researchers to see and photograph ionizing-particle tracks."},{"n":22,"q":"What does a Geiger-Müller counter primarily detect?","opts":["Ionizing radiation events","Magnetic north","Chemical acidity","Sound frequency"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Ionizing radiation events","why":"Radiation ionizes gas in the tube, producing electrical pulses that can be counted."},{"n":23,"q":"Francis Aston’s mass spectrograph was especially important for demonstrating what?","opts":["Many elements contain isotopes with different masses","Every atom has the same mass","Gamma rays are sound waves","Neutrons carry two positive charges"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Many elements contain isotopes with different masses","why":"Aston separated ions by mass-to-charge ratio and measured isotope masses with high precision."},{"n":24,"q":"What is the “mass defect” of a nucleus?","opts":["The difference between the nucleus’s mass and the summed masses of its separated nucleons","The loss of mass caused by weighing errors","The missing electrons in an ion","The amount of chemical impurity"],"a":0,"answer_text":"The difference between the nucleus’s mass and the summed masses of its separated nucleons","why":"The missing mass corresponds to nuclear binding energy through E = mc²."},{"n":25,"q":"What unexpected element did Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann identify after bombarding uranium with neutrons in 1938?","opts":["Barium","Gold","Hydrogen","Lead"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Barium","why":"Barium was far lighter than uranium, creating the puzzle that led to the interpretation of nuclear fission."},{"n":26,"q":"Who provided the physical interpretation that the uranium nucleus had split?","opts":["Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch","Marie Curie and Henri Becquerel","Ernest Lawrence and Harold Urey","James Chadwick and Paul Dirac"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch","why":"Meitner and Frisch explained the result using nuclear deformation and mass-energy conversion."},{"n":27,"q":"The word “fission” was borrowed by Otto Frisch from terminology used in which field?","opts":["Biology","Astronomy","Geology","Economics"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Biology","why":"Biologists used fission for the splitting of a cell; Frisch applied the analogy to a splitting nucleus."},{"n":28,"q":"Which uranium isotope was identified in 1939 as the principal isotope undergoing slow-neutron fission?","opts":["Uranium-235","Uranium-238","Uranium-234","Uranium-232"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Uranium-235","why":"Niels Bohr recognized that the rare isotope uranium-235 accounted for the strong slow-neutron fission response."},{"n":29,"q":"Who conceived the idea of a neutron-driven nuclear chain reaction in 1933?","opts":["Leo Szilard","Werner Heisenberg","Ernest Rutherford","Robert Millikan"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Leo Szilard","why":"Szilard realized that a reaction releasing more than one neutron could reproduce itself and grow."},{"n":30,"q":"Which French-led team reported in 1939 that uranium fission could release additional neutrons, strengthening the chain-reaction idea?","opts":["Frédéric Joliot-Curie, Hans von Halban, and Lew Kowarski","Pierre Curie, Paul Langevin, and Louis de Broglie","Otto Hahn, Max Planck, and Gustav Hertz","Niels Bohr, George Gamow, and Edward Teller"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Frédéric Joliot-Curie, Hans von Halban, and Lew Kowarski","why":"Their measurements showed that fission could produce enough secondary neutrons to make a multiplying system physically plausible."},{"n":31,"q":"Hans Bethe’s 1930s work explained the energy of ordinary stars mainly through what process?","opts":["Nuclear fusion","Chemical combustion","Gravitational friction alone","Radio waves"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Nuclear fusion","why":"Bethe described reaction chains in which light nuclei combine, converting a small amount of mass into stellar energy."},{"n":32,"q":"Which element, atomic number 93, was identified by Edwin McMillan and Philip Abelson in 1940?","opts":["Neptunium","Plutonium","Technetium","Promethium"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Neptunium","why":"Neptunium was the first transuranium element to be definitively identified."},{"n":33,"q":"Which Berkeley team is associated with the 1940-1941 discovery and chemical identification of element 94, plutonium?","opts":["Glenn Seaborg and collaborators","Niels Bohr and collaborators","James Chadwick and collaborators","Otto Hahn and collaborators"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Glenn Seaborg and collaborators","why":"Seaborg, Edwin McMillan, Joseph Kennedy, Arthur Wahl, and colleagues produced and identified the new transuranium element."},{"n":34,"q":"What is the main job of a neutron moderator?","opts":["Slow neutrons through repeated scattering","Give neutrons an electric charge","Convert every neutron into a proton","Block all radiation"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Slow neutrons through repeated scattering","why":"Materials containing light nuclei can reduce neutron energy without necessarily absorbing every neutron."},{"n":35,"q":"Heavy water differs from ordinary water because its hydrogen is largely replaced by what?","opts":["Deuterium","Tritium only","Helium","Lithium"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Deuterium","why":"Heavy water is D₂O, containing hydrogen’s mass-2 isotope."},{"n":36,"q":"Which metal was already known before the Manhattan Project as a strong absorber of slow neutrons?","opts":["Cadmium","Aluminum","Copper","Silver"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Cadmium","why":"Cadmium’s strong neutron absorption made it useful in experiments for controlling or measuring neutron populations."},{"n":37,"q":"George de Hevesy pioneered the use of radioactive isotopes as what?","opts":["Tracers","Mirrors","Electrical insulators","Rocket fuels"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Tracers","why":"A radioactive isotope can follow the chemical behavior of an element while remaining detectable through its radiation."},{"n":38,"q":"Before nuclear weapons, radium was already being used experimentally and medically for what purpose?","opts":["Treating some cancers with radiation","Generating commercial electricity","Powering submarines","Making steel nonmagnetic"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Treating some cancers with radiation","why":"Radium sources were used in early radiation therapy, although protection standards were still developing."},{"n":39,"q":"The Cavendish Laboratory, central to Rutherford and Chadwick’s nuclear research, is part of which university?","opts":["University of Cambridge","University of Chicago","University of Rome","University of Copenhagen"],"a":0,"answer_text":"University of Cambridge","why":"The Cavendish in Cambridge was one of the world’s leading centers of atomic and nuclear physics."},{"n":40,"q":"Niels Bohr built a major theoretical-physics institute in which city?","opts":["Copenhagen","Berlin","Moscow","Tokyo"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Copenhagen","why":"Bohr’s institute became an international meeting place for quantum and nuclear physicists."},{"n":41,"q":"Yoshio Nishina led important prewar nuclear research at RIKEN in which country?","opts":["Japan","Canada","Brazil","Spain"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Japan","why":"Nishina helped establish Japanese experimental nuclear physics and oversaw cyclotron research at RIKEN."},{"n":42,"q":"Which Soviet physicist was closely associated with neutron research and cyclotron development in Leningrad before the war?","opts":["Igor Kurchatov","Andrei Sakharov","Sergei Korolev","Dmitri Mendeleev"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Igor Kurchatov","why":"Kurchatov worked on nuclear physics and helped develop Soviet cyclotron research before later leading the Soviet atomic program."},{"n":43,"q":"Which 1939 message warned President Franklin D. Roosevelt that uranium chain reactions might lead to extremely powerful bombs?","opts":["The Einstein-Szilard letter","The Zimmermann Telegram","The Atlantic Charter","The Bohr correspondence principle"],"a":0,"answer_text":"The Einstein-Szilard letter","why":"Leo Szilard and fellow émigré physicists helped draft the warning, which Albert Einstein signed."},{"n":44,"q":"Which three Hungarian-born physicists were central to urging Einstein to warn the U.S. government in 1939?","opts":["Leo Szilard, Eugene Wigner, and Edward Teller","Niels Bohr, Enrico Fermi, and Hans Bethe","Otto Hahn, Fritz Strassmann, and Werner Heisenberg","John Cockcroft, Ernest Walton, and James Chadwick"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Leo Szilard, Eugene Wigner, and Edward Teller","why":"Szilard drove the initiative, with Wigner and Teller helping reach Einstein and convey the warning."},{"n":45,"q":"What U.S. government body was formed after Roosevelt received the Einstein-Szilard warning?","opts":["Advisory Committee on Uranium","Atomic Energy Commission","National Aeronautics and Space Administration","Strategic Air Command"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Advisory Committee on Uranium","why":"The committee was the first formal U.S. government response to the possibility of uranium chain reactions and bombs."},{"n":46,"q":"Who chaired the original U.S. Advisory Committee on Uranium?","opts":["Lyman Briggs","Vannevar Bush","Leslie Groves","Robert Oppenheimer"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Lyman Briggs","why":"Briggs, director of the National Bureau of Standards, chaired the early committee beginning in 1939."},{"n":47,"q":"Which science administrator became a key organizer of U.S. wartime research and took a leading role in accelerating uranium work before the Manhattan Project?","opts":["Vannevar Bush","J. Edgar Hoover","George Marshall","Henry Stimson"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Vannevar Bush","why":"Bush led the National Defense Research Committee and then the Office of Scientific Research and Development."},{"n":48,"q":"The 1940 Frisch-Peierls memorandum was written at which British university?","opts":["University of Birmingham","University of Oxford","University of Edinburgh","University of Manchester"],"a":0,"answer_text":"University of Birmingham","why":"Otto Frisch and Rudolf Peierls argued at Birmingham that a bomb using separated uranium-235 might be much smaller than many scientists had assumed."},{"n":49,"q":"What British committee evaluated the feasibility of an atomic bomb in 1940-1941?","opts":["MAUD Committee","Tizard Mission","Royal Society Council","Imperial General Staff"],"a":0,"answer_text":"MAUD Committee","why":"The MAUD Committee concluded that a uranium bomb was feasible and recommended an urgent development effort."},{"n":50,"q":"What was the British wartime code name for its atomic-bomb research program beginning in 1941?","opts":["Tube Alloys","Ultra","Mulberry","Chain Home"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Tube Alloys","why":"Tube Alloys concealed the nature of Britain’s secret atomic research and development effort."},{"n":51,"q":"Which Norwegian company operated the major prewar heavy-water production facility at Vemork?","opts":["Norsk Hydro","Siemens","Imperial Chemical Industries","General Electric"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Norsk Hydro","why":"The Vemork plant produced heavy water as a byproduct of industrial hydrogen production."},{"n":52,"q":"When France fell in 1940, Hans von Halban and Lew Kowarski carried a strategically important stock of what material to Britain?","opts":["Heavy water","Pure plutonium","Enriched uranium metal","Radium paint"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Heavy water","why":"The French research team had obtained Norwegian heavy water for chain-reaction experiments and evacuated it to Britain."},{"n":53,"q":"The exceptionally rich Shinkolobwe uranium mine was located in which territory?","opts":["Belgian Congo","Australia","Canada","French Algeria"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Belgian Congo","why":"Ore from Shinkolobwe, controlled by Union Minière du Haut-Katanga, was known for its unusually high uranium concentration."},{"n":54,"q":"What name is commonly given to the German uranium-research group formed after the discovery of fission?","opts":["Uranverein, or Uranium Club","Kaiser Project","Rhine Committee","V-Science Group"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Uranverein, or Uranium Club","why":"German military and scientific authorities organized uranium research in 1939 under the name Uranverein."},{"n":55,"q":"Which physicist became one of the most prominent theoretical figures in Germany’s wartime uranium research?","opts":["Werner Heisenberg","Albert Einstein","Ernest Lawrence","Harold Urey"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Werner Heisenberg","why":"Heisenberg worked on reactor and chain-reaction theory within the German uranium effort."},{"n":56,"q":"What political development after 1933 caused many leading physicists to leave German universities and research institutes?","opts":["Nazi racial and political purges","A ban on all university physics","The closure of every European laboratory","The end of the Nobel Prizes"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Nazi racial and political purges","why":"The dismissal and persecution of Jewish and politically disfavored scholars dispersed an extraordinary concentration of scientific talent."},{"n":57,"q":"Why did Enrico Fermi leave Fascist Italy after receiving the 1938 Nobel Prize?","opts":["Italy’s racial laws endangered his Jewish wife and family","He had been ordered to stop studying neutrons by the Vatican","The University of Rome had closed permanently","He was recruited to lead the German Uranverein"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Italy’s racial laws endangered his Jewish wife and family","why":"Fermi used the Nobel trip as an opportunity to emigrate with his family to the United States."},{"n":58,"q":"After fleeing Nazi Germany in 1938, Lise Meitner continued her work in which country?","opts":["Sweden","Mexico","Japan","Argentina"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Sweden","why":"Meitner escaped to Sweden, where she and Otto Frisch developed the physical explanation of fission."},{"n":59,"q":"What uranium-related action by Germany was mentioned in the 1939 Einstein-Szilard letter as a warning sign?","opts":["Germany had restricted the sale of uranium from Czechoslovak mines under its control","Germany had tested a nuclear weapon","Germany had built a commercial nuclear power station","Germany had outlawed uranium chemistry"],"a":0,"answer_text":"Germany had restricted the sale of uranium from Czechoslovak mines under its control","why":"The letter cited German interest in uranium and restrictions on ore exports as reasons for U.S. concern."},{"n":60,"q":"Which 1914 novel used the phrase “atomic bombs” decades before nuclear fission was discovered?","opts":["The World Set Free by H. G. Wells","Brave New World by Aldous Huxley","The War of the Worlds by H. G. Wells","We by Yevgeny Zamyatin"],"a":0,"answer_text":"The World Set Free by H. G. Wells","why":"Wells imagined long-lived explosive devices powered by atomic energy; the novel later influenced Leo Szilard’s thinking about chain reactions."}];
const TRIVIA_CONTEXT={"1":["In the months after X-rays were announced, a French physicist investigated whether phosphorescent minerals might also produce penetrating rays. He placed uranium salts on wrapped photographic plates, expecting sunlight to be necessary.","Cloudy weather interrupted the planned exposure, but the stored plates still darkened strongly. That accident showed that the effect came from the uranium compound itself rather than from light absorbed beforehand.","These early experiments changed atomic physics by turning unexpected photographic, scattering, and spectral effects into evidence about matter itself."],"2":["The new element was announced in 1898 while its discoverer’s homeland had been divided among neighboring empires and did not exist as an independent state. Naming an element could therefore carry a political message as well as a scientific one.","Marie Curie had been born Maria Skłodowska in Warsaw and retained a strong attachment to the country of her childhood after moving to Paris. The name chosen for the element called attention to that suppressed national identity.","The episode belongs to a period when laboratory observations repeatedly forced physicists to replace familiar models of the atom with more precise ones."],"3":["The Curies began with a dark uranium-bearing mineral left over from mining and refining. Its radioactivity was greater than could be explained by the uranium already known to be present.","They processed enormous quantities of this dense ore residue through repeated crushing, dissolving, precipitating, and measuring. The unexplained activity led them toward two previously unknown radioactive elements.","Its importance lies not only in the discovery but in the experimental reasoning used to distinguish a new physical effect from ordinary light, chemistry, or instrumentation."],"4":["Early investigators noticed that radioactive emissions did not all penetrate matter or bend in fields in the same way. One component was relatively massive and weakly penetrating; another was lighter, more penetrating, and deflected in the opposite direction.","A young physicist working first in Britain and later in Canada gave these two components the first two letters of the Greek alphabet. His later scattering work would also lead to the nuclear atom.","These early experiments changed atomic physics by turning unexpected photographic, scattering, and spectral effects into evidence about matter itself."],"5":["A French experimenter studying radium found a third radiation component that penetrated matter far more strongly than the already known alpha and beta emissions. Magnetic fields did not deflect it as they did charged particles.","The discovery was reported in 1900, before the radiation was widely understood as very energetic electromagnetic radiation. The familiar Greek-letter name was applied after the initial observation.","The episode belongs to a period when laboratory observations repeatedly forced physicists to replace familiar models of the atom with more precise ones."],"6":["Alpha radiation carried a positive charge and was much less penetrating than beta or gamma radiation. By collecting alpha particles and examining their spectrum, researchers connected them to a familiar light element.","The particles carry two units of positive charge and have roughly four atomic mass units. Once neutralized by acquiring electrons, they become ordinary atoms of a noble gas.","Its importance lies not only in the discovery but in the experimental reasoning used to distinguish a new physical effect from ordinary light, chemistry, or instrumentation."],"7":["Most alpha particles sent through very thin metal foil passed almost straight through, but a tiny fraction scattered through surprisingly large angles. Such rare rebounds were difficult to reconcile with a diffuse positive charge.","The result implied that most of the atom is empty space and that the strong electric field is concentrated in a region far smaller than the atom itself. Electrons occupy the surrounding volume.","These early experiments changed atomic physics by turning unexpected photographic, scattering, and spectral effects into evidence about matter itself."],"8":["Classical electrodynamics suggested that an orbiting electron should continuously radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus. It also could not explain why each element produced sharp spectral lines rather than a continuous rainbow.","The 1913 model imposed discrete permitted states. Light was emitted or absorbed only when an electron moved between these states, with the photon energy matching the difference.","The episode belongs to a period when laboratory observations repeatedly forced physicists to replace familiar models of the atom with more precise ones."],"9":["Characteristic X-ray frequencies changed systematically from one element to the next. The pattern was smoother and more fundamental than ordering by atomic weight, which produced several awkward reversals in the periodic table.","The measurements connected each element to the amount of positive charge in its nucleus. This turned the sequence of the periodic table into a physically measurable property.","Its importance lies not only in the discovery but in the experimental reasoning used to distinguish a new physical effect from ordinary light, chemistry, or instrumentation."],"10":["Chemists found atoms that behaved almost identically in chemical reactions yet differed in radioactive behavior or measured mass. They therefore occupied the same place in the periodic table despite not being physically identical.","The shared chemistry reflects the same nuclear charge and electron structure. The differing masses arise from different numbers of neutral nuclear particles.","These early experiments changed atomic physics by turning unexpected photographic, scattering, and spectral effects into evidence about matter itself."],"11":["In 1905, special relativity implied that mass is a concentrated form of energy. The conversion factor involves the square of the speed of light, making even a small mass difference correspond to an enormous amount of energy.","Nuclear reactions later provided dramatic examples because the combined mass of products can differ measurably from that of the initial nuclei. The missing mass appears as kinetic energy or radiation.","The result became part of a broader effort to identify the constituents of nuclei and learn how controlled bombardment could change them."],"12":["In 1919, alpha particles were directed into nitrogen gas. The experiment produced fast hydrogen nuclei and left behind a nucleus with one more positive charge than nitrogen.","Adding an alpha particle contributes two protons, while ejecting a hydrogen nucleus removes one. The final atomic number therefore rises from seven to eight.","This work helped establish the vocabulary of particles, isotopes, transmutation, and acceleration that later nuclear research depended on."],"13":["Rutherford recognized that the nucleus of ordinary hydrogen behaved as a fundamental positively charged building block of heavier nuclei. It was much lighter than an alpha particle and carried one positive elementary charge.","This particle later became paired conceptually with the neutral particle discovered in 1932. Together, the two account for nearly all nuclear mass.","During the 1910s and 1930s, nuclear physics advanced through a close exchange between theory, new particles, and increasingly powerful laboratory machines."],"14":["Beryllium bombarded by alpha particles emitted penetrating radiation that could knock protons out of paraffin. Treating the radiation as gamma rays required implausibly large photon energies.","A Cambridge physicist showed instead that the radiation consisted of neutral particles with nearly the same mass as protons. This resolved a major puzzle in nuclear mass and isotope structure.","The result became part of a broader effort to identify the constituents of nuclei and learn how controlled bombardment could change them."],"15":["Cloud-chamber photographs of cosmic rays showed a track curving like an electron in a magnetic field but in the opposite direction. Its ionization and curvature indicated the same mass as an electron with positive charge.","The observation confirmed a particle predicted by relativistic quantum theory and became the first clear experimental evidence for antimatter. It was made at Caltech using cosmic-ray tracks.","This work helped establish the vocabulary of particles, isotopes, transmutation, and acceleration that later nuclear research depended on."],"16":["Spectroscopic measurements revealed a rare hydrogen isotope with twice the usual mass. Its nucleus contains one charged nuclear particle and one neutral one rather than only the charged particle.","This isotope became important in heavy water, isotope chemistry, and later fusion studies. Its name comes from the Greek idea of “second.”","During the 1910s and 1930s, nuclear physics advanced through a close exchange between theory, new particles, and increasingly powerful laboratory machines."],"17":["The Joliot-Curies found that an aluminum target continued to emit positrons even after the bombarding source had been removed. The target had been transformed into an unstable isotope that decayed on its own.","The incoming projectiles came from a naturally radioactive source and carried two positive charges. Their interaction produced radioactive phosphorus and a neutron.","The result became part of a broader effort to identify the constituents of nuclei and learn how controlled bombardment could change them."],"18":["Fermi’s group noticed that results changed when a neutron source was placed near substances rich in light nuclei. Collisions with those nuclei reduced neutron kinetic energy without removing the neutrons immediately.","For many target nuclei, lower-energy neutrons spend more time near the nucleus and have a larger probability of capture. The apparently accidental placement of paraffin therefore increased some induced activities.","This work helped establish the vocabulary of particles, isotopes, transmutation, and acceleration that later nuclear research depended on."],"19":["The machine used a magnetic field to bend charged particles into repeated semicircular paths. Each time the particles crossed the gap between two hollow electrodes, an alternating voltage accelerated them again.","This repeated acceleration allowed a compact apparatus to reach energies far beyond a single static voltage drop. The particles spiraled outward until extracted toward a target.","During the 1910s and 1930s, nuclear physics advanced through a close exchange between theory, new particles, and increasingly powerful laboratory machines."],"20":["A high-voltage accelerator built in Cambridge sent positively charged hydrogen nuclei into lithium. The reaction produced two energetic alpha particles and offered an early laboratory demonstration of mass-energy conversion.","The projectiles had one positive charge and were much lighter than alpha particles. Neutral projectiles would not have been accelerated by the electric potential used in the apparatus.","The result became part of a broader effort to identify the constituents of nuclei and learn how controlled bombardment could change them."],"21":["This detector contains supersaturated vapor. A charged particle ionizes molecules along its route, and droplets condense on those ions to create a visible line that can be photographed.","Magnetic fields can curve the tracks, allowing researchers to infer charge sign and momentum. Famous photographs of positrons and nuclear reactions relied on this visual method.","This period shows how instruments, quantitative measurements, and international communication combined to make fission and chain reactions scientifically credible."],"22":["The device contains gas between electrodes at high voltage. When radiation ionizes the gas, an avalanche of charge produces a distinct electrical pulse.","Each pulse records an event but generally provides little information about the original particle’s energy. The device is therefore excellent for counting and surveying radiation.","By the late 1930s, improved detectors and isotope measurements allowed researchers to connect small experimental anomalies to major changes in nuclear theory."],"23":["Ions passing through electric and magnetic fields follow paths determined by their mass-to-charge ratios. Photographic traces revealed several distinct masses for atoms that chemists regarded as the same element.","This evidence helped establish that chemical identity does not require a single atomic mass. It also made precise nuclear masses available for binding-energy calculations.","The historical sequence matters: observation came first, competing interpretations followed, and only then did a durable explanation emerge."],"24":["If the masses of separated protons and neutrons are added, the sum is slightly greater than the measured mass of the bound nucleus they form. The difference is not an experimental mistake.","Energy must be supplied to pull the nucleus apart, and the mass difference corresponds to that binding energy through the mass-energy relation. More tightly bound nuclei generally have larger binding energy per nucleon.","This period shows how instruments, quantitative measurements, and international communication combined to make fission and chain reactions scientifically credible."],"25":["After neutron bombardment of uranium, careful chemical separation revealed products that behaved like a familiar alkaline-earth element. The result was astonishing because that element sits far below uranium in atomic mass.","The finding could not be explained as a nearby transuranic element produced by adding one or two particles. It suggested that the uranium nucleus had broken into much smaller pieces.","By the late 1930s, improved detectors and isotope measurements allowed researchers to connect small experimental anomalies to major changes in nuclear theory."],"26":["The chemical evidence came from Berlin, but the decisive physical interpretation was developed by an exiled Austrian physicist and her physicist nephew during the winter of 1938–1939. They used a liquid-drop picture of the nucleus and the mass-energy relation.","They argued that a uranium nucleus could elongate, divide into two large fragments, and release roughly 200 MeV. The nephew then confirmed the process experimentally and helped give it a biological analogy as a name.","The historical sequence matters: observation came first, competing interpretations followed, and only then did a durable explanation emerge."],"27":["When a cell or simple organism divides into two parts, biologists use a short term for that reproductive process. Otto Frisch borrowed the same word because the uranium nucleus appeared to divide into two substantial fragments.","The analogy emphasized splitting rather than radioactive emission of a small particle. It quickly became the standard vocabulary for the newly interpreted nuclear process.","This period shows how instruments, quantitative measurements, and international communication combined to make fission and chain reactions scientifically credible."],"28":["Natural uranium is mostly a heavier isotope, with only a small fraction of a lighter isotope. Bohr’s interpretation linked slow-neutron fission primarily to the rarer component.","The two isotopes have the same chemical behavior, so ordinary chemistry cannot separate them effectively. Their three-unit mass difference instead motivated difficult physical separation methods.","By the late 1930s, improved detectors and isotope measurements allowed researchers to connect small experimental anomalies to major changes in nuclear theory."],"29":["In 1933, a Hungarian-born physicist was crossing a London street when he imagined that one neutron-induced nuclear event might release additional neutrons. If each generation produced more than one successor, the process could grow exponentially.","This insight preceded the discovery of uranium fission, so the first proposed reactions involved other elements. The physicist later patented the chain-reaction concept and became a major advocate of government action.","The historical sequence matters: observation came first, competing interpretations followed, and only then did a durable explanation emerge."],"30":["A Paris laboratory measured not only fission fragments but also neutrons emerging from uranium after fission. The result was essential because a chain reaction requires new neutrons to continue the sequence.","The team combined a prominent French nuclear chemist with two younger physicists who later carried important materials and expertise to Britain after the fall of France.","This period shows how instruments, quantitative measurements, and international communication combined to make fission and chain reactions scientifically credible."],"31":["Ordinary stars shine steadily for billions of years, far longer than chemical burning or gravitational contraction can explain. In the late 1930s, theory showed how light nuclei could combine and convert a small amount of mass into energy.","One important sequence uses carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen nuclei as catalysts while ultimately turning hydrogen into helium. A different proton-proton path dominates in stars like the Sun.","Prewar nuclear science depended on materials, instruments, hospitals, universities, and laboratories distributed across many countries."],"32":["Neutron capture by uranium-238 produces uranium-239, which beta decays to a new element with one more proton than uranium. That element was first identified at Berkeley in 1940.","Its name followed the astronomical naming pattern of uranium, which had been named after Uranus. The next planet outward supplied the new element’s name.","The same nuclear phenomena could serve basic research, medical treatment, industrial tracing, or the study of neutron behavior, depending on the institution and apparatus."],"33":["At Berkeley, element 94 emerged from neutron and deuteron bombardment studies followed by difficult chemical separation. Its chemistry had to be distinguished from neighboring actinide elements before the identification was secure.","The work involved a young chemist who later reorganized the periodic table around the actinide series, together with colleagues including McMillan, Kennedy, and Wahl. The discovery unfolded across 1940 and 1941.","Understanding the setting helps distinguish a scientific discovery from the material or organization that made the work possible."],"34":["Fast neutrons can lose kinetic energy through repeated elastic collisions with light nuclei while remaining available for later reactions. A material selected for this purpose should slow neutrons without absorbing too many of them.","Graphite and heavy water became important candidates in early reactor studies. Their role is different from control materials, which intentionally remove neutrons from the chain.","Prewar nuclear science depended on materials, instruments, hospitals, universities, and laboratories distributed across many countries."],"35":["Ordinary water contains hydrogen nuclei that are almost always single protons. In the heavier form of water, a significant fraction of those nuclei also contain a neutron.","The chemical behavior remains broadly water-like, but the greater nuclear mass changes neutron moderation and absorption properties. This made the material strategically important in early reactor research.","The same nuclear phenomena could serve basic research, medical treatment, industrial tracing, or the study of neutron behavior, depending on the institution and apparatus."],"36":["Early reactor experiments needed materials that could remove neutrons reliably. One metal was found to have a particularly large capture probability for slow neutrons and became useful in control rods and detector shielding.","Thin sheets of the metal also create a sharp cutoff in neutron-energy measurements because low-energy neutrons are strongly absorbed. Its chemical symbol is Cd.","Understanding the setting helps distinguish a scientific discovery from the material or organization that made the work possible."],"37":["A radioactive isotope behaves chemically like the stable atoms of the same element, but its radiation makes it detectable in tiny quantities. Researchers realized that such atoms could be followed through plants, animals, and chemical processes.","The method reveals pathways without requiring enough material to disturb the system significantly. It became important in biology, medicine, and industrial chemistry before the war.","Prewar nuclear science depended on materials, instruments, hospitals, universities, and laboratories distributed across many countries."],"38":["Long before reactors or bombs, physicians placed sealed radioactive sources near diseased tissue. The goal was to damage rapidly dividing cells with ionizing radiation while limiting exposure to surrounding tissue.","Radium needles and tubes were used in early brachytherapy, though safety standards were primitive and medical workers often suffered harmful exposure. The practice grew from the observed biological effects of radiation.","The same nuclear phenomena could serve basic research, medical treatment, industrial tracing, or the study of neutron behavior, depending on the institution and apparatus."],"39":["Rutherford led a famous laboratory where the nucleus, artificial transmutation, and neutron were investigated. The institution had earlier been associated with Maxwell and J. J. Thomson.","It is located in an ancient English university city on the River Cam. Chadwick’s neutron work and many early accelerator experiments occurred within this academic setting.","Understanding the setting helps distinguish a scientific discovery from the material or organization that made the work possible."],"40":["Bohr’s institute became a refuge and meeting place for international theoretical physics between the wars. Visitors discussed quantum mechanics, nuclear structure, and the interpretation of fission in an unusually collaborative atmosphere.","The institute stood in the capital of Denmark and later bore Bohr’s name. Its location also mattered during the German occupation, when Bohr eventually escaped by sea to Sweden.","Prewar nuclear science depended on materials, instruments, hospitals, universities, and laboratories distributed across many countries."],"41":["Yoshio Nishina trained in Europe with Bohr and returned to build a major nuclear-physics program at RIKEN. His group constructed cyclotrons and conducted cosmic-ray and nuclear studies before the Pacific War.","RIKEN was a leading research institute in an East Asian industrial power that also organized wartime uranium investigations. Nishina became one of that country’s best-known modern physicists.","The same nuclear phenomena could serve basic research, medical treatment, industrial tracing, or the study of neutron behavior, depending on the institution and apparatus."],"42":["In Leningrad, a young physicist helped develop cyclotrons and studied neutron-induced reactions during the 1930s. He later became the scientific leader of his country’s wartime and postwar atomic program.","His institute belonged to a rapidly expanding state research system that followed Western nuclear discoveries closely. He is often compared with Oppenheimer as the central organizer of a national bomb effort.","Understanding the setting helps distinguish a scientific discovery from the material or organization that made the work possible."],"43":["In August 1939, a famous theoretical physicist signed a letter drafted with the help of émigré colleagues. It warned that uranium might support a chain reaction and that very powerful bombs could become possible.","The message was delivered to the U.S. president by economist Alexander Sachs. It helped prompt the first federal uranium committee, though government action remained limited at first.","Between 1939 and 1941, nuclear questions moved from academic journals into government committees, confidential memoranda, and wartime research organizations."],"44":["The effort to alert Washington was driven largely by three physicists who had grown up in the same Central European country and later emigrated. They feared that Nazi Germany might exploit fission first.","One originated the chain-reaction idea, one was a major mathematical physicist, and one later became closely associated with thermonuclear weapons. They approached Einstein because his fame could command presidential attention.","Different institutions evaluated scientific feasibility, military implications, and the practical resources required for further work."],"45":["Roosevelt’s first formal response to the uranium warning was a small committee rather than a massive development program. It included military representatives and civilian scientists and considered funding experiments in graphite, uranium, and chain reactions.","The body was initially housed under the National Bureau of Standards and predated both the National Defense Research Committee’s larger role and the Manhattan Engineer District.","The names and dates matter because these early bodies formed a sequence rather than one single program appearing fully formed."],"46":["The first federal uranium committee was led by the director of the National Bureau of Standards. He was a physicist known for research in terrestrial magnetism and electrical standards rather than a bomb designer.","His cautious administrative style frustrated some scientists who wanted faster action. Leadership later shifted toward broader wartime research organizations under more forceful science administrators.","Between 1939 and 1941, nuclear questions moved from academic journals into government committees, confidential memoranda, and wartime research organizations."],"47":["This engineer and science administrator had led the Carnegie Institution of Washington and promoted an analog calculating machine called the differential analyzer. In 1940 he became head of a new organization coordinating defense research.","He gained direct access to Roosevelt and helped move uranium work from scattered committees toward a large, centrally managed national project. His administrative influence extended across radar, medicine, and weapons research.","Different institutions evaluated scientific feasibility, military implications, and the practical resources required for further work."],"48":["Two refugee physicists in Britain wrote a short memorandum arguing that a bomb made from separated uranium-235 might require far less material than previously assumed. Their estimate transformed British perceptions of feasibility.","They were working at a university in England’s industrial Midlands, where one of them held a position under physicist Mark Oliphant. The city had a major manufacturing history and was heavily bombed during the war.","The names and dates matter because these early bodies formed a sequence rather than one single program appearing fully formed."],"49":["Britain created a secret expert body to assess the Frisch-Peierls conclusions. It brought together physicists, chemists, and industrial advisers and issued reports in 1941 supporting an urgent bomb program.","The committee’s unusual name was deliberately opaque. A later legend connected it to a garbled telegram about a governess, although the exact origin remains debated.","Between 1939 and 1941, nuclear questions moved from academic journals into government committees, confidential memoranda, and wartime research organizations."],"50":["After favorable committee reports, Britain organized a secret atomic research and development effort in 1941. Its code name was intentionally meaningless and sounded more like industrial hardware than nuclear physics.","The program investigated isotope separation, reactors, and weapon feasibility. British work later merged substantially with the American project under wartime agreements.","Different institutions evaluated scientific feasibility, military implications, and the practical resources required for further work."],"51":["A hydroelectric industrial complex at Vemork produced fertilizer by electrolyzing water. Repeated electrolysis concentrated the heavier form of water as a byproduct.","The plant belonged to a major Norwegian chemical and energy company founded around hydroelectric power. During the German occupation, its heavy-water production became the target of famous sabotage operations.","The episode illustrates how nuclear science was already international and political before it became a centralized American wartime project."],"52":["French researchers had acquired a rare moderator material from Norway for chain-reaction experiments. As German forces advanced in 1940, the team evacuated the stock through southern France and carried it to Britain.","The liquid was valuable because it could slow neutrons while absorbing relatively few of them. At the time, it represented much of the world’s available supply.","European war, occupation, migration, and control of strategic resources reshaped nuclear research before the Manhattan Project formally began."],"53":["The Shinkolobwe mine produced ore with uranium concentrations far higher than typical deposits in North America or Europe. Its output was controlled by Union Minière du Haut-Katanga.","The mine lay in a central African colony ruled by Belgium. Colonial ownership and secret stockpiles later gave the United States access to exceptionally rich ore.","Scientists, documents, minerals, and specialized materials crossed borders under intense political pressure, linking national programs that were otherwise secretive."],"54":["German physicists and officials organized several rounds of uranium research beginning in 1939. The group studied reactors, isotope separation, moderators, and the possibility of nuclear explosives.","Its common German nickname combines the word for uranium with a term meaning association or club. English-language histories often translate it directly.","The episode illustrates how nuclear science was already international and political before it became a centralized American wartime project."],"55":["Germany’s uranium effort included a Nobel-winning theorist famous for matrix mechanics and the uncertainty principle. He analyzed neutron multiplication and led reactor experiments using uranium and heavy water.","After the war, debate continued over his intentions and the technical direction of the German program. Regardless of interpretation, he was its most internationally prominent theoretical participant.","European war, occupation, migration, and control of strategic resources reshaped nuclear research before the Manhattan Project formally began."],"56":["Beginning in 1933, German universities dismissed scholars under racial laws and political loyalty rules. Jewish scientists and opponents of the regime lost positions, citizenship, and personal safety.","The exodus dispersed leading physicists, chemists, and mathematicians to Britain, Scandinavia, and the United States. Many later contributed to Allied nuclear research and other wartime science.","Scientists, documents, minerals, and specialized materials crossed borders under intense political pressure, linking national programs that were otherwise secretive."],"57":["The Italian physicist traveled to Stockholm in December 1938 to receive the Nobel Prize. Instead of returning to Rome, he and his family continued to the United States.","His wife, Laura, came from a Jewish family, and new Fascist racial legislation threatened her and their children. The Nobel ceremony provided a safe and publicly plausible route out of the country.","The episode illustrates how nuclear science was already international and political before it became a centralized American wartime project."],"58":["The Austrian physicist had worked for decades in Berlin but was forced to flee after the Nazi annexation of Austria removed her remaining legal protection. Colleagues helped her cross borders secretly in 1938.","She obtained a research position in a neutral Scandinavian country, though with fewer resources than she had enjoyed in Berlin. From there she exchanged letters with Hahn and met her nephew during the Christmas holiday when they interpreted fission.","European war, occupation, migration, and control of strategic resources reshaped nuclear research before the Manhattan Project formally began."],"59":["The 1939 warning to Roosevelt pointed not only to scientific publications but also to signs of strategic interest in uranium. Germany had recently gained control over mining regions in occupied Czechoslovakia.","The letter noted that sales from those mines had been curtailed. Such restrictions suggested that the material might be regarded as strategically valuable rather than an ordinary commercial commodity.","Scientists, documents, minerals, and specialized materials crossed borders under intense political pressure, linking national programs that were otherwise secretive."],"60":["A British writer published a novel in 1914 imagining that artificial radioactivity would produce weapons releasing energy over long periods. He used language strikingly similar to terminology adopted decades later.","The story follows war, social collapse, and the eventual creation of a world government. Leo Szilard later recalled that the book helped shape his thinking about atomic energy and chain reactions.","The episode illustrates how nuclear science was already international and political before it became a centralized American wartime project."]};
